{% when 'attribution-identity' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">ATTRIBUTION & IDENTITY</span><h1>How a click becomes a defensible attribution link</h1><p>Separate acquisition identity from commerce identity and connect records with defensible evidence.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>6–8 MIN READ</span><span>EDUCATIONAL GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Two different identities</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Capture the original identifiers</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Connect in evidence order</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / What if a link is missing?</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Attribution is not causation</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Two different identities</h2><p>Attribution identity describes where acquisition activity originated. Commerce identity describes which commercial transaction an event belongs to. A network click ID, network transaction ID, and merchant order ID should not be collapsed into a single generic identifier.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Click ID → Network conversion ID → Merchant order reference"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Click ID</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Network conversion ID</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Merchant order reference</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Capture the original identifiers</h2><p>Keep original provider identifiers and raw source values alongside normalized fields where supported. Exact provider IDs, shared TraceKit identifiers, merchant order references, and parent-child transaction IDs provide stronger evidence than similarity in amounts or timing.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Click ID • Affiliate ID • Transaction ID • Order ID</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Connect in evidence order</h2><p>Prefer exact shared identifiers and deterministic provider references. Then inspect other known relationships. Heuristic matching can inform an investigation, but it should not be silently presented as a verified source relationship.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Exact ID → Known relationship → Unresolved"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Exact ID</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Known relationship</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Unresolved</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What if a link is missing?</h2><p>Preserve the unmatched purchase or conversion with its source evidence. Missing attribution must not prevent commerce ingestion or force a guess. Keep the unresolved state visible until stronger evidence is available.</p><blockquote>Unknown is a valid investigative outcome.</blockquote></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Attribution is not causation</h2><p>A recorded attribution relationship does not prove that the acquisition touch caused the purchase. Incrementality requires additional evidence. Describe what the data actually supports.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>An attributed order is not automatically an incremental order.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how these concepts relate to TraceKit's product and the evidence behind customer journeys.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'customer-journeys-guide' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">CUSTOMER JOURNEYS</span><h1>What happens after the first purchase?</h1><p>Follow upsells, rebills, refunds, and disputes without flattening the commercial lifecycle.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>6–8 MIN READ</span><span>EDUCATIONAL GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / A journey is more than a conversion</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Preserve the event structure</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / An illustrative example</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / Follow the evidence</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Investigate downstream outcomes</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>A journey is more than a conversion</h2><p>The initial purchase is only one event. Post-purchase offers, subscription charges, refunds, disputes, and adjustments can occur later and in different systems.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Click → Base order → Upsell → Rebill → Refund"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Click</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Base order</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Upsell</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 04</small><strong>Rebill</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 05</small><strong>Refund</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Preserve the event structure</h2><p>Keep base orders, upsells, downsells, warranties, subscriptions, rebills, refunds, and disputes as distinct source and financial events. Connect them through exact references or known parent-child relationships.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>One customer can have many distinct commerce events.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>An illustrative example</h2><p>A fictional customer buys a $129 product, adds a $49 upsell, then receives a $35 partial refund. Purchases total $178 and collected revenue after this refund is $143 before other costs. This is not net profit.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="$129 + $49 − $35 = $143"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>$129 + $49 − $35 = $143</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Follow the evidence</h2><p>Use merchant order IDs, provider transaction IDs, subscription references, and other deterministic links. Customer-level similarity alone should not prove that one transaction is the child of another.</p><blockquote>Keep both attribution and commerce identities.</blockquote></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Investigate downstream outcomes</h2><p>Ask which acquisitions are associated with repeat purchases, which orders were refunded, and which disputes remain unresolved. Avoid claiming more coverage than connected source records support.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Missing events should remain visible, not silently filled in.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how these concepts relate to TraceKit's product and the evidence behind customer journeys.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'data-reconciliation' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">DATA RECONCILIATION</span><h1>Why two dashboards show different numbers</h1><p>Investigate mismatches across attribution, orders, payments, and financial records.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>6–8 MIN READ</span><span>EDUCATIONAL GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Different systems count different events</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Align the comparison</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Compare records, not just totals</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / Keep unresolved records</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Explain the gap</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Different systems count different events</h2><p>A network may count conversions, a store may count created orders, and a payment processor may count captured payments. Those totals can differ without any system being broken.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Conversions ≠ Orders ≠ Captures"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Conversions ≠ Orders ≠ Captures</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Align the comparison</h2><p>Confirm identical reporting windows, time zones, currencies, status definitions, test/live scope, and treatment of upsells, rebills, reversals, and refunds before comparing totals.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Scope → Status → Currency → Time</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Compare records, not just totals</h2><p>Use exact source IDs to identify missing, duplicated, delayed, and unmatched records. Preserve raw and normalized values to support later review.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Exact identifiers outrank guesswork."><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Exact identifiers outrank guesswork.</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Keep unresolved records</h2><p>An order without attribution should remain an order. A network conversion without a matching commerce record should remain visible. Dropping either makes a report appear complete at the cost of accuracy.</p><blockquote>Unmatched does not mean invalid.</blockquote></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Explain the gap</h2><p>Record the source, time window, event type, identifiers, status, and suspected reason for each exception. Revisit delayed records without overwriting original evidence.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>A useful reconciliation explains why totals differ.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how these concepts relate to TraceKit's product and the evidence behind customer journeys.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'profit-financial-outcomes' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">FINANCIAL OUTCOMES</span><h1>Revenue isn't the same as profit</h1><p>See how refunds, fees, advertising, and fulfillment affect the financial picture.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>6–8 MIN READ</span><span>EDUCATIONAL GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Define the metric</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Keep adjustments separate</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / An illustrative calculation</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / Don't invent missing costs</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Follow financial outcomes over time</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Define the metric</h2><p>Gross sales, collected revenue, contribution margin, and net profit answer different questions. The calculation should disclose what is included and excluded.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Sales → Collected revenue → Contribution"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Sales</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Collected revenue</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Contribution</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Keep adjustments separate</h2><p>Refunds, disputes, chargebacks, fees, and other financial adjustments are distinct events with their own source references and status. A pending dispute is not necessarily a final loss.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>A refund should not erase the original order.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>An illustrative calculation</h2><p>Suppose fictional purchases total $10,000, refunds are $600, advertising spend is $2,400, fulfillment is $700, and payment fees are $300. Collected revenue is $9,400; the remainder after those listed costs is $6,000. Other expenses are excluded.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="$10,000 − $600 − $2,400 − $700 − $300 = $6,000"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>$10,000 − $600 − $2,400 − $700 − $300 = $6,000</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Don't invent missing costs</h2><p>If advertising, shipping, fees, or dispute outcomes are unavailable, report known components and label the missing ones. An incomplete contribution figure should not be called net profit.</p><blockquote>Unknown cost coverage must remain explicit.</blockquote></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Follow financial outcomes over time</h2><p>Use source records to investigate which sales were adjusted, which campaigns are associated with refunded orders, and how rebills affect collected revenue.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Financial conclusions depend on source coverage.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how these concepts relate to TraceKit's product and the evidence behind customer journeys.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'tracekit-intelligence-guide' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">TRACEKIT INTELLIGENCE</span><h1>From a business question to supporting evidence</h1><p>Investigate business records while keeping source relationships and uncertainty visible.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>6–8 MIN READ</span><span>EDUCATIONAL GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Ask a precise question</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Follow the evidence</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Show unresolved findings</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / Respect permissions</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Built-in and external Intelligence</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Ask a precise question</h2><p>Start with a specific business question, such as which affiliates are associated with disputes or why conversions differ from orders. Define the time period and data scope.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Question → Source records → Evidence"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Question</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Source records</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Evidence</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Follow the evidence</h2><p>A defensible answer should identify the supporting source records and distinguish commerce identity from attribution identity. Correlation is not proof of causation.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Explain what supports each conclusion.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Show unresolved findings</h2><p>Imagine a fictional set of 12 disputes: seven linked to Affiliate A, three to Affiliate B, and two unattributed. The two unresolved cases are part of the answer, not a gap to hide.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="7 matched + 3 matched + 2 unresolved = 12"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>7 matched + 3 matched + 2 unresolved = 12</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Respect permissions</h2><p>Data visibility and governed actions depend on account, organization, client context, and permissions. The presence of a tool does not grant every user authority to execute it.</p><blockquote>Governed access is part of trust.</blockquote></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Built-in and external Intelligence</h2><p>TraceKit Intelligence is intended for use within the product. Compatible external clients may access supported governed tools via MCP when configured and permitted. Availability depends on production readiness.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>MCP is optional, not required.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how these concepts relate to TraceKit's product and the evidence behind customer journeys.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'integrations-mcp-guide' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">INTEGRATIONS & MCP</span><h1>Connecting systems without losing context</h1><p>Understand supported connector coverage, preserved identifiers, and governed AI extensions.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>6–8 MIN READ</span><span>EDUCATIONAL GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / What a connection means</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Preserve provider evidence</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Verify readiness</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / How MCP fits</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Plan your onboarding</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What a connection means</h2><p>An ecosystem listing does not guarantee a production-ready connector. Confirm each provider's supported events, access scope, sync behavior, and rollout stage.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Featured platform ≠ universally available connector"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Featured platform ≠ universally available connector</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Preserve provider evidence</h2><p>Keep original source IDs and raw provider values alongside normalized forms where supported. Distinct orders, refunds, rebills, and disputes should not be flattened.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Raw source → Normalized record → Relationships</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Verify readiness</h2><p>Confirm authorization, supported topics, historical scope, sync freshness, failures, and unresolved mapping behavior. A successful login alone does not prove ingestion coverage.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Authorize → Verify → Reconcile"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Authorize</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Verify</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Reconcile</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>How MCP fits</h2><p>MCP enables compatible external assistants to discover supported tools. Authentication, client context, permissions, and production policy govern actual availability and execution.</p><blockquote>Tool discovery is not action approval.</blockquote></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Plan your onboarding</h2><p>List the questions you want answered, the sources holding relevant evidence, and the exact event types required. Test representative records and document gaps before expanding.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Start with evidence coverage, not logo count.</p></div></section>
